bplist00�XUTI-Data�[public.htmlOlE<meta charset='utf-8'><html><head></head><body><h1><span>MASTER PROMPT: CREATE AN AWARD-WINNING WEB DEVELOPER PROFILE WEBSITE</span></h1><h2><span>1. Your Role</span></h2><p><span>Act as a world-class UI/UX designer, creative director, senior frontend engineer, motion designer, and personal-brand strategist.</span></p><p><span>Create a complete, visually stunning, production-quality personal portfolio website for a professional Web Developer / Software Engineer. This must not look like a generic portfolio template, a basic resume website, or an ordinary SaaS landing page.</span></p><p><span>The website must communicate technical expertise, creativity, professionalism, attention to detail, and real-world software development experience.</span></p><p><strong><span>Primary requirement:</span></strong><span> I will upload my CV/resume as a PDF, DOCX, or other supported document. Analyze the uploaded CV and use its actual information to build my complete professional profile. Extract my name, professional title, summary, skills, employment history, projects, education, certifications, achievements, and contact details where available.</span></p><p><span>Do not invent experience, qualifications, clients, project statistics, testimonials, awards, or technologies that are not supported by the CV or additional information I provide.</span></p><p><span>If some information is missing, omit it gracefully or leave an editable placeholder.</span></p><h2><span>2. Creative Direction: "The Digital Craftsperson"</span></h2><p><span>Build a distinctive personal website that feels like a fusion of:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>An award-winning independent developer portfolio.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A premium editorial publication.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A sophisticated interactive digital resume.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A modern software engineer's creative workspace.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A carefully art-directed personal brand.</span></p></li></ul><p><span>The design should feel custom-made for a talented web developer, not generated from a common portfolio template.</span></p><p><span>Create a strong visual identity with an intentional grid, exceptional typography, asymmetrical layouts, refined spacing, elegant transitions, and a memorable first impression.</span></p><p><span>Avoid excessive gradients, generic purple AI aesthetics, unnecessary glassmorphism, repetitive rounded cards, emoji-based icons, and excessive animation.</span></p><h3><span>Visual identity</span></h3><p><span>Use the following creative direction:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>Background: deep charcoal, near-black, or midnight navy.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Primary text: warm white or soft ivory.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Accent: electric lime or acid green, used selectively.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Secondary accent: muted silver or cool gray.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Optional supporting color: subtle cobalt blue for interactive states.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Typography: pair a distinctive editorial display font with a clean modern sans-serif.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Code details: use a refined monospace font for technical labels, metadata, and developer-inspired elements.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Borders: thin, low-contrast lines that establish structure.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Layout: spacious, architectural, and editorial, with occasional oversized typography.</span></p></li></ul><p><span>Use a consistent design system for colors, typography, spacing, shadows, borders, buttons, and animation.</span></p><p><span>The finished website must look premium in both desktop and mobile layouts.</span></p><h2><span>3. Analyze the Uploaded CV</span></h2><p><span>Before designing the website, thoroughly inspect the uploaded CV.</span></p><p><span>Extract and organize the information into these categories:</span></p><ol start="1"><li><p><span>Full name and preferred professional identity.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Current professional title.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Professional summary.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Years of experience, only when supported by the CV.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Core programming languages.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Frameworks and libraries.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Databases and data technologies.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Tools, deployment platforms, and development environments.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Employment history and responsibilities.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Selected projects and project descriptions.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Technical contributions and business impact.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Education and certifications.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Professional contact information.</span></p></li><li><p><span>GitHub, LinkedIn, personal website, and other verified links.</span></p></li></ol><p><span>Prioritize actual accomplishments and responsibilities over generic claims.</span></p><p><span>Rewrite raw CV content into concise, professional, compelling website copy while preserving factual accuracy.</span></p><p><span>For projects, explain the problem, the solution, the technologies used, and the functionality delivered whenever the source information supports it.</span></p><p><span>Do not simply paste the entire CV onto the website. Transform it into a polished digital narrative.</span></p><h2><span>4. Website Structure</span></h2><p><span>Create a complete single-page portfolio with the following sections and navigation.</span></p><h3><span>A. Premium Navigation</span></h3><p><span>Create a compact, elegant navigation bar.</span></p><p><span>Include:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>Personal wordmark based on my name or initials.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Navigation links: Home, About, Experience, Projects, Skills, Contact.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A visible "Download CV" action.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A small availability indicator only if the CV or my supplied information confirms it.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A responsive mobile navigation menu.</span></p></li></ul><p><span>The navigation should remain accessible while scrolling. Use a transparent background initially and a subtle solid background or border after scrolling if supported by the implementation.</span></p><p><span>Add refined hover states, active section indicators, and smooth scrolling.</span></p><h3><span>B. Hero Section — The First Impression</span></h3><p><span>The hero section must be visually unforgettable.</span></p><p><span>Use a bold, editorial composition with large typography, intentional whitespace, and a distinctive visual element.</span></p><p><span>Include:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>A small introductory label such as "SOFTWARE ENGINEER / WEB DEVELOPER".</span></p></li><li><p><span>My name in oversized, carefully arranged typography.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A concise, powerful professional headline derived from the CV.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A short paragraph explaining what I build and the problems I solve.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Primary CTA: "Explore My Work".</span></p></li><li><p><span>Secondary CTA: "Download CV".</span></p></li><li><p><span>A GitHub or LinkedIn link if available.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A compact technical signature featuring a few verified core technologies.</span></p></li></ul><p><span>Use a layout that balances typography with a visual focal point.</span></p><p><span>Possible signature elements:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>A custom animated code window displaying a short, meaningful code snippet.</span></p></li><li><p><span>An abstract geometric composition inspired by software architecture.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A refined typographic monogram.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A subtle interactive grid or terminal-inspired element.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A floating project preview integrated into the composition.</span></p></li></ul><p><span>Choose one primary visual concept and execute it beautifully. Do not combine every idea.</span></p><p><span>The hero should feel bespoke and memorable without becoming cluttered.</span></p><p><span>Use subtle entrance animations with staggered text reveals, understated movement, and a carefully timed appearance of the CTA buttons.</span></p><p><span>Respect reduced-motion accessibility preferences.</span></p><h3><span>C. Professional Introduction</span></h3><p><span>Create an About section that tells my professional story.</span></p><p><span>Include:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>A concise professional introduction.</span></p></li><li><p><span>My development philosophy.</span></p></li><li><p><span>The types of software I build.</span></p></li><li><p><span>My approach to clean code, maintainability, performance, security, and user experience, when consistent with my actual background.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A few factual highlights from my CV.</span></p></li></ul><p><span>Present the content using an editorial layout rather than a standard biography card.</span></p><p><span>Use a mixture of large text, small metadata labels, subtle separators, and asymmetric columns.</span></p><p><span>If a professional portrait is available, integrate it elegantly without making it dominate the website. Otherwise, use a custom typographic or abstract visual instead of inventing a personal photograph.</span></p><h3><span>D. Selected Projects — The Main Attraction</span></h3><p><span>This should be one of the most visually impressive sections.</span></p><p><span>Read the projects listed in my CV and any additional project links I provide. Build a curated showcase that highlights the strongest and most relevant work first.</span></p><p><span>For every project, include the available information:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>Project name.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Short, compelling description.</span></p></li><li><p><span>My role or contribution.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Technologies used.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Important functionality.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A project screenshot or genuine visual preview, when available.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A live demo link, if verified.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A source code link, if available and public.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Project category, such as SaaS, management system, ecommerce, healthcare, logistics, or other relevant categories.</span></p></li></ul><p><span>Design project presentations as large editorial feature blocks rather than identical cards.</span></p><p><span>Use a varied composition:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>One prominent featured project with a large visual preview.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Secondary projects arranged in an asymmetric grid.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Distinctive project metadata.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Small technology labels.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Elegant hover interactions.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A clear link to explore each project.</span></p></li></ul><p><span>Every project must have a different visual identity appropriate to its actual purpose.</span></p><p><span>For example, a healthcare management system could use a clean clinical interface preview, an ecommerce project could feature a polished product interface, and a logistics application could use a shipment-tracking dashboard.</span></p><p><span>Use genuine screenshots or real project assets whenever possible. Never fabricate a screenshot and represent it as an actual live product.</span></p><p><span>If screenshots are unavailable, create an explicitly illustrative interface mockup or a tasteful abstract visual that does not falsely claim to be a real screenshot.</span></p><p><span>If I supply a projects page URL, inspect the available information and extract additional verified project details where possible.</span></p><p><span>If there are many projects, initially show the strongest selection and provide a functional "View All Projects" interaction.</span></p><p><span>Project filtering is optional and should only be included if it improves usability.</span></p><h3><span>E. Professional Experience</span></h3><p><span>Transform my employment history into an elegant career timeline.</span></p><p><span>For each position, show:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>Company name.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Job title.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Employment dates.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Location, if available.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Main responsibilities.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Notable contributions.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Technologies used, when supported.</span></p></li></ul><p><span>Use a sophisticated vertical timeline or editorial list with a thin connecting line, restrained accent markers, and clean typography.</span></p><p><span>Highlight meaningful engineering work rather than presenting long blocks of text.</span></p><p><span>Keep each role concise and scannable.</span></p><p><span>Make the progression of my career clear without exaggerating seniority or accomplishments.</span></p><h3><span>F. Technical Skills — Developer Capability Map</span></h3><p><span>Create a beautifully organized technical skills section.</span></p><p><span>Extract all relevant technologies from the CV and group them into appropriate categories, for example:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>Programming Languages.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Backend Development.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Frontend Development.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Databases.</span></p></li><li><p><span>APIs and Integrations.</span></p></li><li><p><span>DevOps and Deployment.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Development Tools.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Testing and Quality Assurance.</span></p></li></ul><p><span>Only show categories and technologies that are supported by the source material.</span></p><p><span>Use visually refined skill labels, typography, icons from a consistent icon library, and optional subtle interactions.</span></p><p><span>Avoid generic percentage-based skill bars such as "PHP 95%" unless a defensible, explicitly supplied measurement exists.</span></p><p><span>Do not assign fake proficiency scores.</span></p><p><span>The section should communicate breadth, practical experience, and technical focus without resembling a gaming dashboard.</span></p><h3><span>G. Education and Certifications</span></h3><p><span>If available in my CV, display education and certifications in a compact, polished section.</span></p><p><span>Include:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>Degree or qualification.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Institution.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Graduation year or duration.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Relevant certification details.</span></p></li></ul><p><span>Use understated styling and clear hierarchy.</span></p><p><span>Do not create fictional certifications or academic credentials.</span></p><h3><span>H. Developer Principles / Working Approach</span></h3><p><span>Create a short section highlighting my approach to engineering.</span></p><p><span>Possible themes include:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>Maintainable architecture.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Reliable application behavior.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Secure authentication and authorization.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Database efficiency.</span></p></li><li><p><span>API design.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Responsive interfaces.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Practical problem-solving.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Collaboration and requirements analysis.</span></p></li></ul><p><span>Choose themes that align with the uploaded CV.</span></p><p><span>Present them as a small number of thoughtfully designed principles, supported by concise explanations.</span></p><p><span>Do not fill this section with generic marketing statements.</span></p><h3><span>I. Contact Section — A Strong Closing</span></h3><p><span>Design a visually compelling closing section that encourages recruiters, clients, and collaborators to contact me.</span></p><p><span>Include:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>A bold headline.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A short, professional invitation.</span></p></li><li><p><span>My verified email address.</span></p></li><li><p><span>LinkedIn and GitHub links, if available.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Other verified professional contact channels.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A clear contact CTA.</span></p></li></ul><p><span>A functional contact form may be included if a working backend or email service is available. Include validation, loading states, success feedback, and error handling.</span></p><p><span>If there is no backend, provide a functional email link and do not pretend that a contact form sends messages.</span></p><p><span>Do not publish private information from the CV that is not appropriate for public display.</span></p><h3><span>J. Footer</span></h3><p><span>Create a minimal but memorable footer.</span></p><p><span>Include:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>My name or personal wordmark.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Copyright year generated dynamically.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Links to the major sections.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Verified professional social links.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A subtle developer-inspired signature, such as "Designed with intention. Built with code."</span></p></li></ul><p><span>Keep it refined and uncluttered.</span></p><h2><span>5. Distinctive Interactive Features</span></h2><p><span>Add a small number of meaningful interactions that make the website memorable.</span></p><p><span>Prioritize the following:</span></p><ol start="1"><li><p><span>Smooth scrolling between sections.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Subtle reveal animations as sections enter the viewport.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Custom project hover previews.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Animated hero typography.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Interactive navigation states.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A functional CV download.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Optional light/dark theme toggle if both themes are fully designed.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A back-to-top control when useful.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Optional keyboard-accessible project details or a modal.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A refined custom cursor effect on desktop only if it does not interfere with normal pointer behavior.</span></p></li></ol><p><span>All interactions must work correctly.</span></p><p><span>Avoid excessive parallax, constant motion, animated backgrounds that compete with content, and effects that reduce readability.</span></p><p><span>Make sure the website remains usable with touch input, a keyboard, and reduced-motion settings.</span></p><h2><span>6. CV Upload and Content Management</span></h2><p><span>If the environment supports backend development and persistent storage, implement an administrative interface for managing my profile.</span></p><p><span>The administrative interface should support:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>Uploading a replacement CV.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Extracting and previewing document content.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Reviewing extracted information before publication.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Editing my name, headline, biography, and contact information.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Adding, editing, reordering, and removing projects.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Updating experience and skills.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Managing live project links and GitHub repositories.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Uploading project screenshots and a profile image.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Downloading the current public CV.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Publishing approved profile changes.</span></p></li></ul><p><span>CV replacement must not automatically overwrite the published website with unreviewed or incorrectly extracted data. Provide a review-and-confirm step.</span></p><p><span>Validate file types and sizes. Protect administrative actions with authentication and authorization. Store uploaded files securely and do not expose administrative uploads publicly by default.</span></p><p><span>If a backend is not available, build the portfolio from the uploaded CV and implement the available editing features using the environment's supported capabilities. Clearly distinguish local editing from persistent, server-side content management.</span></p><h2><span>7. CV Download Requirements</span></h2><p><span>The "Download CV" button must work.</span></p><p><span>Use the actual uploaded CV as the downloadable document when possible.</span></p><p><span>Requirements:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>Use a clear filename based on my name.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Ensure the file is accessible from the public profile.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Do not link to a nonexistent file.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Provide a visible fallback if the original CV is not yet available.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Keep the public download separate from the administrative upload process.</span></p></li></ul><p><span>If the website supports both an uploaded CV and a generated web resume, keep the uploaded CV as the default download unless I explicitly choose otherwise.</span></p><h2><span>8. Technical Implementation</span></h2><p><span>Use the strongest technology stack supported by the target environment.</span></p><p><span>Preferred frontend:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>React or Next.js.</span></p></li><li><p><span>TypeScript.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Tailwind CSS or a similarly maintainable styling system.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Framer Motion or a lightweight animation alternative.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Lucide icons or another consistent icon library.</span></p></li></ul><p><span>If the environment is a plain HTML/CSS/JavaScript artifact builder, implement the experience using the supported technologies rather than introducing incompatible dependencies.</span></p><p><span>Use reusable components and a clear project structure.</span></p><p><span>Recommended component organization:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>Navigation</span></p></li><li><p><span>Hero</span></p></li><li><p><span>About</span></p></li><li><p><span>FeaturedProjects</span></p></li><li><p><span>ProjectItem</span></p></li><li><p><span>ExperienceTimeline</span></p></li><li><p><span>Skills</span></p></li><li><p><span>Education</span></p></li><li><p><span>Contact</span></p></li><li><p><span>Footer</span></p></li><li><p><span>CVDownload</span></p></li></ul><p><span>Keep profile content in a structured data model so that projects, skills, and experience can be updated without rewriting the page layout.</span></p><p><span>Avoid unnecessary dependencies and oversized animation libraries.</span></p><p><span>Write maintainable, well-organized code.</span></p><h2><span>9. Responsive Design</span></h2><p><span>Design and test the entire experience at these approximate widths:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>Large desktop: 1440px and above.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Standard laptop: 1024–1439px.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Tablet: 768–1023px.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Mobile: 375–767px.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Small mobile: 320–374px.</span></p></li></ul><p><span>Ensure:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>No horizontal overflow.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Headlines scale appropriately.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Project layouts adapt naturally.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Navigation works on mobile.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Buttons have comfortable touch targets.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Images maintain appropriate aspect ratios.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Text remains readable.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Interactive elements remain accessible.</span></p></li></ul><p><span>Do not simply shrink the desktop layout. Create a considered mobile experience.</span></p><h2><span>10. Accessibility, SEO, and Performance</span></h2><p><span>Implement:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>Semantic HTML.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Accessible navigation and buttons.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Descriptive image alt text.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Keyboard navigation.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Visible focus states.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Appropriate color contrast.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Reduced-motion support.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Optimized images.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Lazy loading where appropriate.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Descriptive page titles and meta descriptions.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Open Graph metadata.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A favicon based on my personal brand.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Canonical metadata when the final domain is known.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Structured data for a person, using verified information.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A custom 404 page if supported.</span></p></li></ul><p><span>Avoid unnecessary scripts, layout shifts, oversized images, and excessive font loading.</span></p><p><span>Aim for strong Core Web Vitals and high Lighthouse accessibility, SEO, and performance scores. Measure actual results rather than claiming scores without testing.</span></p><h2><span>11. Final Quality Standard</span></h2><p><span>The final website must feel like a premium, bespoke developer portfolio worthy of sharing with recruiters, engineering managers, international clients, and potential collaborators.</span></p><p><span>It must have:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>A memorable visual identity.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Exceptional typography and spacing.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Strong content hierarchy.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Clear professional positioning.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Attractive project storytelling.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A coherent design system.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Smooth, purposeful interactions.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Fully functional links and controls.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Responsive layouts.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Accurate CV-based information.</span></p></li><li><p><span>A working CV download.</span></p></li><li><p><span>No placeholder text accidentally left visible.</span></p></li><li><p><span>No fake statistics, fabricated reviews, or invented accomplishments.</span></p></li><li><p><span>No broken images or dead buttons.</span></p></li></ul><p><span>Do not stop after generating a concept, wireframe, or static hero section. Build the complete experience with every requested section.</span></p><p><span>Before finishing, inspect the entire website, test all navigation and download actions, check mobile layouts, verify project URLs, and resolve any visible errors.</span></p><h2><span>12. Execution Instructions</span></h2><p><span>Work in this order:</span></p><ol start="1"><li><p><span>Read the uploaded CV and extract the profile information.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Identify the strongest professional positioning and most impressive projects.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Establish the visual direction and design system.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Build the complete portfolio.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Add interactions, animations, and responsive behavior.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Connect the actual CV download.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Verify all supplied links and factual claims.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Test the website and fix issues.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Deliver the finished website or runnable project.</span></p></li></ol><p><span>Use creative judgment to make design decisions without asking me to choose every color, font, or animation.</span></p><p><span>The objective is not merely to display my resume online. The objective is to create a distinctive professional identity that makes people remember my name, understand my engineering capabilities, explore my work, and feel confident contacting me.</span></p><p><strong><span>Final instruction: Make it exceptional, original, visually sophisticated, technically sound, and genuinely usable.</span></strong></p></body></html>␀␀␀␀␀#␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀ll